A||||6||Cooter||Clutch ideas?||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||08-26-2000||09:54 PM||||I have been helping a friend try to get the hydraulics working on his 88 gt. We have replaced the slave 3 times- the bleeder screw boss broke off the first two! And the master cylinder twice and still cannot get it to release correctly. I wondered about a bent pedal, but am not sure. We are not getting any more air out of it. The slave is the cast one- is this where I need the steel tube type? If so, where can we get one?||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||08-26-2000||10:17 PM||||This is exactly what happened to me last year with an '88 Formula.  I broke the boss off of 2 aftermarket slaves and the third one had a bad seal.  I will again reitterate my feelings on aftermarket slaves (and masters).....THEY ARE JUNK!!!!!<P>Run, don't walk, to your nearest GM dealer and buy a genuine GM slave cylinder.  It will cost more than those aftermarket ones, but I guarantee you will be happy.  <P><p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 08-26-2000).]||||reg||11
Z||000002||ur2biteme||08-27-2000||06:43 AM||||i've been having the hydraulic blues as well.  well, until i found that my damn STEEL pedal was bent.  i have an 87 4cyl, and don't rule that out.  take a look.  the bending only has to be slight to screw up the angles down there.  i replaced mine, without re-bleeding, and i instantly had a clutch again.  of course i bleeded it again for good measure.<P>btw, thought it would be funny to mention, but i saw a fiero at the local pic-n-pull yard with a spotless ALUMINUM pedal.  that's what i'm using right now.  yeah.  i replaced it with the inferior one.  oh well, it works||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||08-27-2000||07:20 AM||||Give a quick to the M/C pushrod to pedal fit there should be NO slop. If the hole is even slightly elongated you might have a bent steel pedal(ask me how I know).||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ralph||08-27-2000||07:22 AM||||Ther is a lot to be learned about the Fiero hydraulic clutch. Some good ideas are posted by Ed Parks from "The Fiero Factory". There is a section there to help you with Clutch Problems.<BR>Look on <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#top" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#top</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick||08-27-2000||02:50 PM||||<BR>Ok Phil, I'll bite. How do you know?||||reg||6
Z||000006||GT Bastard||08-27-2000||03:13 PM||||I just replaced my slave as well... A friend of mine has had several 4sp Fiero's and has gone through several aftermarket and GM slaves.  The GM units crapped-out faster than the aftermarkets.||||reg||1
